@node Error handling
@chapter Error handling

libdtfl separates two kinds of failure: @emph{errors}, which a caller is
expected to handle, and @emph{contract violations}, which are bugs.

@section Error codes

A fallible function returns @code{0} (@code{DTFL_OK}) on success, or a
non-zero @code{DTFL_E*} code:

@table @code
@item DTFL_EINVAL
an invalid argument;
@item DTFL_ENOMEM
an allocation failed (only from a hook that may fail);
@item DTFL_ERANGE
a value outside a representable range;
@item DTFL_ESTATE
the operation is not valid in the current state;
@item DTFL_EALGEBRA
the diff class lacks an operation the operator needs
(@pxref{Collections});
@item DTFL_EIO
an I/O or network failure;
@item DTFL_EPROTO
malformed wire data;
@item DTFL_ENOTSUP
not supported in this build;
@item DTFL_EOF
end of stream.
@end table

@code{dtfl_strerror} returns a static description of a code.
@code{dtfl_last_error_detail} returns a human-readable string with
context for the most recent error on the calling thread, or an empty
string if there is none; it is valid until the next libdtfl call on the
same thread.  The buffer is per OS thread, so under a fiber runtime the
fibers on one carrier thread share it.  A host callback that returns a
code to libdtfl, such as a transport returning @code{DTFL_EIO}, sets
the reason with @code{dtfl_set_error_detail}, which takes a
@code{printf} format.  A typical check:

@example
if (dtfl_execute(cfg, worker, ctx, NULL) != 0)
        errx(1, "dtfl: %s", dtfl_last_error_detail());
@end example

@section Contract violations and the panic hook

A contract violation (for example, using a capability for the wrong
output, or stepping a worker from inside an operator) is a programming
error, not a runtime condition.  It calls the @dfn{panic hook}, which
must not return: the default prints the message to standard error and
calls @code{abort}.  A host installs its own with
@code{dtfl_set_panic_hook} to redirect the failure, for example a
PostgreSQL extension that calls @code{ereport(ERROR)} to @code{longjmp}
out of the computation.  Allocation failure also goes through the panic
hook.

@section The five host hooks

The panic hook is one of five process-wide hooks that route libdtfl's
boundaries through a host program.  All five have defaults, so a
standalone program needs none of them, and all five must be installed
before the first @code{dtfl_execute}.

@table @code
@item dtfl_set_alloc_hooks
All memory libdtfl allocates comes from these @code{malloc}/@code{realloc}/
@code{free} callbacks.  The default is the libc allocator.  This hook
must be set before any libdtfl call allocates, and at most once.

@item dtfl_set_panic_hook
The contract-violation and allocation-failure handler described above.

@item dtfl_set_log_hook
Receives @emph{human-readable} diagnostics at @code{DTFL_LOG_DEBUG},
@code{DTFL_LOG_INFO}, @code{DTFL_LOG_WARN} and @code{DTFL_LOG_ERROR}.
With no hook set, libdtfl writes WARN and ERROR to standard error and
drops the rest, so a library is quiet by default.  This is separate from
the structured event logging (@pxref{Diagnostics}), which carries
machine-readable events and is unaffected by this hook.

@item dtfl_set_thread_hooks
Spawn and join callbacks for the worker, network and connection threads.
With no hooks set, libdtfl uses pthreads.  A host that manages its own
threads routes thread creation through these; each hook must start a
thread in the same process, since the workers of one execution share
memory.  A host that cannot spawn threads at all, such as a PostgreSQL
backend, drives a single worker cooperatively instead
(@pxref{Embedding}).

@item dtfl_set_sync_hooks
Mutexes, condition variables, the futex-style wait and wake each worker
parks on, yield, sleep, the monotonic clock and the thread identity.
Every lock and wait libdtfl makes goes through these, so a fiber runtime
can keep its carrier threads out of the kernel.  The defaults are
pthreads and, on Linux, @code{futex}.  The hooks latch on the first lock
or clock read, so set them before any other libdtfl call.
@end table
